# Project Rules

## Core Principles

- Files should be easy to open, scan, and change.
- Route or feature ownership is the default; shared placement is reserved for code with real reuse.
- Modules should be small and cohesive, without one-line wrappers or abstract folders.
- Framework conventions come first. Project conventions should clarify ownership or boundaries.

## App Layout

```
app/
├── api/                  # Route handlers
├── <route>/              # App Router segment
│   ├── page.tsx          # Public route entry
│   ├── layout.tsx        # Segment layout when needed
│   ├── loading.tsx       # Segment loading UI when needed
│   ├── error.tsx         # Segment error boundary when needed
│   ├── _components/      # Route-private UI
│   ├── _hooks/           # Route-private React hooks
│   ├── _lib/             # Route-private logic, helpers, constants
│   ├── _types/           # Route-private types
│   └── _features/        # Route-private feature modules for larger routes
│       └── <feature>/
│           ├── _components/
│           ├── _hooks/
│           ├── _lib/
│           └── _types/
├── components/           # Shared UI used by multiple routes
├── hooks/                # Shared hooks used by multiple routes
├── lib/                  # Shared runtime-safe utilities and integrations
│   └── server/           # Server-only utilities and integrations
└── types/                # Shared type declarations
```

This repo uses root `app/`. A `src/` directory belongs only in an intentional whole-app migration.

## Colocation

- Route-owned code belongs in that route's `_components`, `_hooks`, `_lib`, or `_types` folder.
- Large routes may group cohesive route-owned areas under `_features/<feature>/{_components,_hooks,_lib,_types}`.
- Shared folders (`app/components`, `app/hooks`, `app/lib`, `app/types`) are for code used by multiple routes or code that is clearly route-independent.
- Each module should have one canonical owner. Shared code belongs in shared folders, while route-owned copies should exist only as documented temporary shims.
- Avoid parallel old/new directory trees for the same responsibility. Imports should resolve to the canonical location for that code.
- Private folders (`_folder`) are for implementation details inside `app/`.
- Route groups (`(group)`) are only for organizing routes or layouts without changing URLs.
- API route handlers should stay thin: parse the request, call server logic, and return a response.

## Naming

- Filenames are `kebab-case`, including component files: `chat-message.tsx`, `theme-toggle.tsx`, `model-viewer-state.ts`.
- Next.js special filenames stay exact: `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `route.ts`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`.
- Exported React component names and TypeScript interfaces use `PascalCase`.
- Functions, variables, and hook names use `camelCase`. Hook filenames start with `use-`.
- Files are named after the main thing they contain. Vague names like `utils.ts`, `helpers.ts`, or `common.ts` are only acceptable in very small scopes.

## File Size And Extraction

- Normal source files should stay around 150-250 lines when practical.
- Files above 300 lines should have a clear reason to remain together or a meaningful split point, such as a component, hook, parser, state machine, data mapper, or domain helper.
- Extracted code should have a clear responsibility, a useful name, or real reuse. Five-line extraction without a stronger reason is noise.
- React components should focus on rendering and interaction. Reusable stateful behavior belongs in hooks, and pure logic belongs in `_lib`.

## Imports And Boundaries

- Imports from the project root use the `@/` path alias.
- Imports belong at the top of the file. `next/dynamic` is for intentional client-side code splitting.
- Barrel files are not part of the project structure. Import directly from the source module.
- Types should not be re-exported through components or hooks. Import types from `_types` or `app/types`.
- Type-only imports use `import type`.
- Code in `app/lib` should be safe for both Server and Client Components unless it lives under `app/lib/server`.
- Code in `app/lib/server` is server-only and must not be imported by Client Components.

## React And Next.js

- Server Components are the default. `"use client"` is for browser APIs, state, effects, event handlers, or client-only libraries.
- Client Components should be as small as practical and receive only the data they need.
- Data fetching belongs on the server when possible. Independent async work should use `Promise.all`.
- Values derived from props or state should usually be computed during render instead of synced in effects.
- Refs are for transient values that should not trigger renders.
- State updates that depend on previous state should use functional updates.
- Memoization should have a measured or obvious render cost.

## TypeScript And Code Quality

- Strict mode is enabled. Do not weaken types to make errors disappear.
- Do not use `any`. If a cast is unavoidable, cast to a specific type.
- Type-only imports use `import type`.
- Object shapes use interfaces. Unions and intersections use type aliases.
- Discriminated union switches must handle every case or have a `never` default.
- Early returns are preferred over nested conditionals.
- Repeated ID or membership lookups should use `Map` or `Set`.
- Comments should explain non-obvious intent, constraints, or trade-offs, not narrate the code.
- Edits should stay focused and avoid rewriting unrelated code.
